    "outcome_summary": "Using ICILS 2018 data on 8th-grade students (N=40,403) and CIL plausible values, the study regressed students’ digital competence on gender, age, immigrant background, and ICT-related background variables, comparing a baseline model to extended models that add three non-self-reported non-cognitive proxies. The proxies—drop in response time, drop in performance, and item response rate—were significant predictors of CIL scores, with drop in response time emerging as the strongest. After accounting for these non-cognitive proxies, the gender and socioeconomic gaps in digital skills decreased, while the immigrant-native gap also changed in the opposite direction to the authors’ expectation. The paper used survey design weights and replicate weights for standard errors and combined results across plausible values using Rubin’s rules.",
